<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Vyazma</h1><p class="st-meta">1239 CE – 1403 CE (approx.) · capital Vyazma</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A small appanage of the Smolensk lands on the road to Moscow, taken by Lithuania in 1403.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyazma">Vyazma</a>, on the upper Vyazma river east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a>, is first mentioned in the 13th century as a town of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-smolensk">Principality of Smolensk</a>. In the mid 13th century it became a separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> held by a junior line of the Smolensk princes, the ancestors of the Vyazemsky family.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its importance came from its position on the main route between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, which made it a trading post and a frontier fortress. Little is known of its individual rulers or institutions, and it was usually subordinate to Smolensk.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great">Vytautas</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vyazma">Vyazma</a> in 1403 in the course of his conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> lands, and its princes became Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a>. The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-moscow">Grand Duchy of Moscow</a> captured it in 1494, and the Vyazemsky princes entered Russian service.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="principality, 1239 CE – 1403 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">principality</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:84.36%;width:3.26%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1239 CE – 1403 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vyazma</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Viazma Principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1321&amp;focus=principality-of-vyazma" aria-label="Principality of Vyazma on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5431 292 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Vyazma lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5751 548L5705 559M5751 548L5556 562"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5705 559h0M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5751 548h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5751 548h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vyazma. <a href="/atlas?year=1321&amp;focus=principality-of-vyazma">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
